      "content": "# Coding Standards\n\nRead this document before generating any Python code for a Universal Extension. These rules apply to all generated code \u2014 actions, utilities, helpers, and formatters.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OOP Pattern \u2014 Wrap Related Functions in Classes\n\nAll generated code follows an OOP pattern. Related functions are grouped into classes, state is held as instance attributes, and private helpers use `self` instead of receiving state as explicit arguments.\n\n```python\n# Good \u2014 class groups state and behaviour together\nclass RunPipeline:\n    def __init__(self, input_data: InputFields) -> None:\n        self._input = input_data\n        self._client = PipelineClient(input_data)\n        self._output_fields = OutputFields()\n\n    def execute(self) -> ActionOutput:\n        result = self._submit()\n        self._output_fields.update(pipeline_state=result.state.name)\n        return ActionOutput(pipeline_run_id=result.name)\n\n    def _submit(self) -> PipelineResult:\n        return self._client.submit(\n            display_name=self._input.display_name,\n            template_path=self._input.template_path,\n        )\n\n# Bad \u2014 module-level function threading state through arguments\ndef run_pipeline(input_data: InputFields) -> ActionOutput:\n    credentials = _build_credentials(input_data)\n    client = _build_client(credentials, input_data.project_id, input_data.region)\n    result = _submit(client, input_data.display_name, input_data.template_path)\n    ...\n```\n\nSee `actions.md` for the full action class pattern and `credentials.md` for the credentials handler pattern.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Constants as Class Attributes\n\nConstants belong to the class that uses them. Module-level constants are a last resort reserved for values that are genuinely shared across multiple unrelated classes.\n\n```python\n# Good \u2014 scoped to the class that uses them\nclass PipelineFormatter:\n    _TABLE_FMT = \"rounded_outline\"\n    _SUMMARY_